<example xml:id="example-stokes-cylinder">
<title>A Circulation That Is Easier as a Flux</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y^2\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + z^2\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
where <m>C</m> is the curve in which the plane <m>y + z = 2</m> cuts the
cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, traversed counterclockwise when seen from
above. The curve is drawn in
<xref ref="fig-stokes-cylinder-tikz"/>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Parametrizing <m>C</m> directly is possible but unpleasant. Instead
take <m>S</m> to be the flat region that <m>C</m> bounds in the plane,
the graph of <m>z = 2 - y</m> over the unit disk <m>R</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-stokes-cylinder-tikz"/>. For a graph <m>z = g(x,y)</m>
with an upward normal, <xref ref="eq-flux-param"/> gives
<m>\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma = \langle -g_x, -g_y, 1\rangle\,dx\,dy</m>;
check that upward is what the right-hand rule pairs with a
counterclockwise <m>C</m>. Compute the curl, dot, and integrate over
the disk; one term vanishes by symmetry.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$S$: \qquad $\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$\nabla\times\mathbf F =$ \qquad $(\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}\,d\sigma =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r = \iint_R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the surface and its normal, for the curl and the integrand, and for the double integral over the disk.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-cylinder-tikz">
<caption>
The curve <m>C</m> of <xref ref="example-stokes-cylinder"/>: the plane
<m>y + z = 2</m> cuts the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> in an ellipse.
The shaded elliptical region is the surface <m>S</m> used in the
solution, and its shadow is the unit disk <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-cylinder" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A vertical cylinder cut by a slanted plane, with the ellipse of
intersection and its shadow on the horizontal plane.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent vertical cylinder of radius one stands on the horizontal
plane. A slanted plane cuts it in an ellipse drawn in vermillion, with
arrowheads showing counterclockwise travel seen from above. The
elliptical region inside the curve is shaded blue, and directly below
it the unit disk in the x y plane is shaded gray as its shadow.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{18},
      width=11.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!70, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=-1.5, xmax=1.5, ymin=-1.5, ymax=1.5, zmin=0, zmax=3.4,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- the unit disk R in the xy-plane (the shadow) ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=black!30, fill=black!12,
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:1, samples=14, y domain=0:360, samples y=40,
              forget plot]
      ({x*cos(y)}, {x*sin(y)}, 0);
    % ---- the cylinder wall, up to the cutting plane ----
