<subsection xml:id="sec-stokes-conceptual">
<title>A Conceptual Stokes' Theorem Problem</title>

<p>
The following problem uses no computation at all. It tests whether you
can see what Stokes' theorem is really saying about circulation, curl,
and<mdash/>as the extension questions bring out<mdash/>the role played
by the domain of the vector field.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-stokes-conceptual">
<title>Equal Curls, Equal Circulations</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and <m>\mathbf{G}</m> be continuously
differentiable vector fields defined on all of <m>\R^3</m>. Suppose
that
</p>
<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \nabla \times \mathbf{G}
</me>
<p>
everywhere in <m>\R^3</m>. Without computing any line integrals,
determine whether the statement
</p>
<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \oint_C \mathbf{G} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
</me>
<p>
is true for every smooth simple closed curve <m>C</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
If the statement is true, prove it using Stokes' theorem,
<xref ref="thm-stokes"/>. If it is false, provide a counterexample.
</p>
<p>
(Consider <m>\mathbf H = \mathbf F - \mathbf G</m>. Check, component by
component, that the curl distributes over subtraction, so that
<m>\nabla\times\mathbf H</m> is known. Then take any oriented surface
<m>S</m> bounded by <m>C</m> <mdash/> why does such a surface lie in
the domain of the fields? <mdash/> and apply <xref ref="thm-stokes"/>
to <m>\mathbf H</m>. Finish with the linearity of the line integral.
Say where the hypothesis that the domain is all of <m>\R^3</m> was
used, and give a second argument through conservative fields and the
loop property of <xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,16);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12.5) -- (15,12.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,8.5) -- (15,8.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5.5) -- (15,5.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,15.7) {true or false? \qquad $\nabla\times(\mathbf F - \mathbf G) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.2) {Stokes' theorem applied to $\mathbf H$ on a surface $S$ bounded by $C$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.2) {by linearity of the line integral:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.2) {where the domain mattered:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {the same conclusion via conservative fields:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for the verdict and the curl of the difference, for the application of Stokes' theorem, for the linearity step, for the role of the domain, and for the alternative argument through conservative fields.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-stokes-conceptual-extensions">
<title>Extension Questions</title>
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Does the conclusion remain valid if the common domain is an arbitrary
open subset of <m>\R^3</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Which hypothesis of Stokes' theorem is essential in the proof?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
How is the result related to conservative vector fields and the
Fundamental Theorem for line integrals of
<xref ref="sec-ftli" text="title"/>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {(a)};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {(b)};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {(c)};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three labelled rows, one for each extension question.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
